// Part 3 — entity, payroll, transfer-tax, exempt-organization and representation forms.
export const FORMS_PART3 = [
form("1065","U.S. Return of Partnership Income","Business entities","Partnership information return","Reports a partnership's income, deductions, gains, losses and other tax items. A partnership generally is a pass-through entity: it calculates and reports items but ordinarily does not pay federal income tax at the entity level.","Domestic partnerships and certain foreign partnerships with U.S. filing obligations. A domestic partnership generally files unless it has neither income nor expenditures treated as deductions or credits for federal tax purposes.","The partnership files Form 1065 and prepares a Schedule K-1 (Form 1065) for each partner. Partners generally report their distributive shares on their own returns, whether or not cash was distributed. Certain international items may require Schedules K-2 and K-3.","A partner's distributive share is determined under the partnership agreement and tax-allocation rules, subject to substantial-economic-effect and other Code requirements. Separately stated items retain their character when passed through. Guaranteed payments are generally ordinary income to the recipient and may affect self-employment tax.","Calendar-year partnership returns are generally due March 15 (the 15th day of the 3rd month after year-end); Form 7004 generally provides an automatic 6-month filing extension. Extension does not extend payment deadlines for partner-level tax.","A partnership earns $120,000 ordinary business income and has two equal partners. Each generally receives a K-1 reporting a $60,000 distributive share, subject to agreement terms and special allocations. A partner may owe tax on the share even if the partnership retains the cash.","Form 1065 is generally informational at entity level. Follow the K-1 to each partner; distinguish allocations, distributions, guaranteed payments, basis, at-risk and passive-loss limits.","partnership;pass-through;Schedule K-1;guaranteed payments;distributive share;basis","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1065",["Partnership","Pass-through"]),
form("Schedule K-1 (1065)","Partner's Share of Income, Deductions, Credits, etc.","Business entities","Partner-level tax allocation","Reports each partner's allocated share of partnership tax items, including ordinary business income, interest, dividends, capital gains, deductions, credits and other separately stated items.","The partnership prepares a K-1 for each partner and furnishes it to that partner. The partner uses it to prepare the individual, corporate, trust or other applicable return.","Amounts flow to the partner's return according to their character. Ordinary business income may flow to Schedule E; interest and dividends retain their character; credits and deductions flow to their designated forms or schedules.","A K-1 is not a cash statement. Taxable allocations can arise without a cash distribution. Partner basis, at-risk rules, passive activity rules and other limitations may restrict deductions or losses.","Generally furnished by the Form 1065 due date, including extensions. Calendar-year partnerships generally have a March 15 due date.","A partner receives a K-1 showing $18,000 ordinary business income and a $2,000 cash distribution. The income allocation is generally taxable even though only $2,000 was distributed; basis and other limitations must be reviewed separately.","Separate K-1 tax allocations from cash distributions. A partner's outside basis is essential for loss deductibility and gain/loss on distributions or sale.","K-1;partner;basis;pass-through;Schedule E;separately stated items","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-k-1-form-1065",["Partnership","K-1"]),
form("1120","U.S. Corporation Income Tax Return","Business entities","C corporation income tax return","Reports a domestic C corporation's taxable income, deductions, credits, tax and payments. Unlike a partnership or S corporation, a C corporation is generally a separate federal income-taxpaying entity.","Domestic corporations and certain foreign corporations with U.S. income or filing obligations. An LLC may be taxed as a corporation if it elects corporate classification or is classified as one by default under applicable rules.","The corporation reports income and deductions on Form 1120 and pays its corporate tax. Dividends paid to shareholders are generally reported separately to shareholders, often on Form 1099-DIV, and may be taxed again at shareholder level.","A C corporation is generally subject to the 21% federal corporate income tax rate on taxable income. Corporate deductions, credits, NOL limitations, accounting methods and special rules can alter the computation. Shareholder wages and dividends must be distinguished.","For a calendar-year corporation, Form 1120 is generally due April 15 (15th day of the 4th month after year-end). Form 7004 generally requests an automatic 6-month extension to file, not to pay.","A C corporation has $500,000 taxable income before federal income tax. At a 21% rate, tentative regular corporate tax is $105,000 before credits and other adjustments. A later dividend can create shareholder-level tax.","C corporation = entity-level tax. Watch for possible double taxation of distributed earnings; do not apply pass-through K-1 treatment to a C corporation shareholder.","C corporation;double taxation;21 percent;Form 7004;dividend;entity-level tax","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1120",["C corporation","Entity-level tax"]),
form("1120-S","U.S. Income Tax Return for an S Corporation","Business entities","S corporation information return","Reports an eligible corporation's income, deductions, credits and other tax items. An S corporation generally passes taxable items through to shareholders, while certain taxes may still be imposed at entity level.","A corporation that has made a valid S election and meets eligibility requirements, including shareholder and stock-class restrictions. Form 2553 is used to elect S corporation status.","The S corporation files Form 1120-S and issues Schedule K-1 (1120-S) to each shareholder. Shareholders report pass-through items on their returns. Shareholder-employees generally receive reasonable compensation reported on Form W-2 before non-wage distributions are considered.","S corporation income generally passes through and is taxed to shareholders whether distributed or not. Shareholders generally cannot deduct losses beyond stock/debt basis and other limitations. Reasonable compensation is a major compliance issue; distributions do not replace wages for services.","Calendar-year Form 1120-S is generally due March 15. Form 7004 generally provides an automatic 6-month extension to file. K-1s should be furnished by the return due date, including extensions where applicable.","An S corporation has $100,000 ordinary business income after paying its shareholder-employee reasonable wages. The remaining pass-through income is reported on K-1 and may be subject to shareholder-level income tax, but is generally not self-employment income merely because it passes through.","Contrast S corp and partnership: S corp shareholder distributions generally are not self-employment earnings, but reasonable W-2 wages are required for shareholder-employees. Basis and distribution ordering matter.","S corporation;pass-through;reasonable compensation;K-1;Form 2553;shareholder basis","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1120-s",["S corporation","Pass-through"]),
form("Schedule K-1 (1120-S)","Shareholder's Share of Income, Deductions, Credits, etc.","Business entities","S corporation shareholder allocation","Reports an S corporation shareholder's share of income, deductions, credits and other tax items, including separately stated items and basis-relevant information.","The S corporation prepares one K-1 for each shareholder and furnishes it to the shareholder. The shareholder uses it with Form 1040 or another applicable return.","Ordinary business income generally flows to Schedule E; separately stated items retain their tax character and may flow to Schedule D, Form 4797, Schedule A or other forms as applicable.","K-1 income generally passes through whether distributed or not. Shareholder losses are limited by stock and debt basis, at-risk rules, passive activity rules and excess business loss rules where applicable. Distributions can be tax-free only to the extent permitted by basis and ordering rules.","Generally furnished by the Form 1120-S due date. Calendar-year S corporations generally have a March 15 due date.","A shareholder receives $30,000 of ordinary business income on K-1 and no cash distribution. The amount is generally included in the shareholder's tax computation despite no cash receipt, subject to applicable rules.","Do not equate K-1 income with distributions. Track stock basis, direct shareholder debt basis, distributions, nondeductible expenses and loss limitations.","S corporation K-1;shareholder;stock basis;debt basis;Schedule E;pass-through","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-k-1-form-1120-s",["S corporation","K-1"]),
form("940","Employer's Annual Federal Unemployment (FUTA) Tax Return","Payroll","Annual federal unemployment tax return","Reports the employer's annual Federal Unemployment Tax Act liability. FUTA funds federal and state workforce programs; it is generally an employer-paid tax, not withheld from employee wages.","Employers who paid wages of $1,500 or more in any calendar quarter, or had one or more employees for at least some part of a day in 20 or more different weeks in the current or preceding year, generally must file, subject to agricultural, household and other special rules.","The employer calculates FUTA on the first $7,000 of each employee's wages, applies applicable credits for state unemployment contributions and reports the annual liability. FUTA deposits are made separately when required; Form 940 reconciles the year.","The statutory FUTA rate is 6.0% on the first $7,000 of each employee's wages. A credit of up to 5.4% may generally be available for qualifying state unemployment contributions, producing a common net rate of 0.6%; credit-reduction states can increase liability.","Generally due January 31 after the calendar year. If all FUTA tax was deposited timely, the filing deadline is generally February 10. Deposit rules generally require a deposit when quarterly accumulated FUTA liability exceeds $500.","An employee earns $40,000. Only the first $7,000 is in the FUTA wage base. At a common net 0.6% rate, the basic FUTA amount is $42 before any special credit-reduction effects.","FUTA is employer-paid and annual. Do not confuse it with FICA, which includes employee withholding and employer matching, or with state unemployment tax.","FUTA;unemployment;employer tax;7000 wage base;credit reduction;annual return","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-940",["Payroll","Annual filing"]),
form("941","Employer's Quarterly Federal Tax Return","Payroll","Quarterly payroll tax return","Reports federal income tax withheld from employees and both employee and employer shares of Social Security and Medicare taxes, along with adjustments and deposits.","Most employers paying wages subject to federal income tax withholding or Social Security and Medicare taxes file Form 941 quarterly. Employers specifically notified by the IRS to file Form 944 generally use that annual return instead.","Payroll records and Forms W-4 determine withholding. The employer reports wages, tips, withholding and FICA liability on Form 941, makes tax deposits under its assigned deposit schedule, and reconciles deposits with the quarterly liability.","For 2026, Social Security tax is generally 6.2% each for employee and employer up to the annual wage base ($184,500); Medicare is generally 1.45% each with no wage cap. Additional Medicare Tax of 0.9% is withheld from an employee after wages exceed $200,000 from that employer; it has no employer match.","Quarterly due dates are generally April 30, July 31, October 31 and January 31 for the preceding quarters, with next-business-day rules when dates fall on weekends/holidays. Timely depositors may have a short additional filing window in certain cases.","For $5,000 of Social Security and Medicare wages below the Social Security cap, employee FICA withholding is generally $382.50 (7.65%) and employer matching is another $382.50, before income-tax withholding or other adjustments.","941 is a quarterly return, but tax deposits may be monthly or semiweekly. Filing and depositing are separate duties. The employer portion is not withheld from employee take-home pay.","Form 941;quarterly;FICA;withholding;Social Security wage base;Medicare","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-941",["Payroll","Quarterly filing"]),
form("944","Employer's Annual Federal Tax Return","Payroll","Annual payroll tax return by IRS designation","Allows certain very small employers to report federal income tax withheld and Social Security and Medicare taxes annually rather than quarterly.","Only employers whose expected annual employment tax liability is $1,000 or less and who have been notified by the IRS to file Form 944 generally may use it. An employer should not switch from Form 941 to Form 944 solely on its own judgment.","The employer reports annual wages, withholding and FICA liability on Form 944 and makes deposits according to the applicable deposit schedule. If the IRS directs the employer to file Form 941, quarterly filing applies.","Form 944 changes the reporting frequency, not the underlying withholding or FICA tax rules. Deposit obligations still apply; an annual filer may still need to deposit during the year depending on liability and deposit rules.","Generally due January 31 after the calendar year; if all tax was deposited timely, the filing deadline is generally February 10. IRS authorization is required to use Form 944.","A tiny employer estimates annual employment taxes of $800 but has not received IRS authorization for Form 944. It should continue filing Form 941 unless the IRS instructs otherwise.","Exam trap: $1,000 or less is an eligibility criterion, but IRS notification is essential. Form 944 is not a free election available to every small employer.","Form 944;annual payroll;IRS notification;Form 941;employment taxes","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-944",["Payroll","Annual filing"]),
form("709","United States Gift (and Generation-Skipping Transfer) Tax Return","Transfer taxes","Gift tax reporting return","Reports certain gifts and generation-skipping transfers made during the calendar year. The donor generally is responsible for gift tax reporting and any gift tax due.","An individual donor generally files when gifts to a donee exceed the annual exclusion, gifts of future interests are made, certain gift-splitting elections are made, or other filing triggers apply. Certain transfers to a spouse or charity have special rules.","The donor reports gifts and available exclusions/deductions on Form 709. Taxable gifts generally reduce the donor's remaining lifetime estate-and-gift basic exclusion amount; gift tax may be due after available exclusion is exhausted. The return is separate from Form 1040.","The annual exclusion generally applies per donor, per donee, per calendar year, for qualifying present-interest gifts. Direct payment of qualifying tuition to an educational institution or medical expenses to a provider may qualify for separate exclusions. Gift splitting requires consent and reporting rules.","Generally due April 15 of the year after the gift. An extension to file the donor's income tax return generally extends the Form 709 due date, but not payment of gift tax. For 2026, the annual exclusion is $19,000 per donee; the basic exclusion amount is $15,000,000. Verify special elections and donor eligibility in current instructions.","A donor gives one friend $25,000 in 2026. The 2026 annual exclusion is $19,000, so the excess $6,000 is generally a taxable gift reported on Form 709 and uses part of the donor's $15,000,000 basic exclusion; it does not automatically mean $6,000 of gift tax is payable.","A taxable gift is not necessarily a gift tax due. Separate annual exclusion, lifetime exclusion, gift splitting, marital/charitable deductions and direct tuition/medical payments.","gift tax;annual exclusion;unified credit;gift splitting;generation-skipping;Form 709","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-709",["Gift tax","Transfer taxes"]),
form("706","United States Estate (and Generation-Skipping Transfer) Tax Return","Transfer taxes","Estate tax return","Reports a decedent's gross estate, allowable deductions, taxable estate, adjusted taxable gifts and estate tax computation. It may also be used to elect portability of a deceased spouse's unused exclusion when requirements are met.","The executor or personal representative generally files when the gross estate, plus adjusted taxable gifts and specific statutory adjustments, exceeds the applicable basic exclusion amount, or when a portability election is desired and permitted. Special nonresident and other rules may apply.","The estate gathers asset valuations, ownership interests, deductions and prior taxable gifts. Form 706 computes any estate tax and can elect portability. Beneficiaries generally do not report inherited property as ordinary income merely because it is inherited, though later income and sales can be taxable.","Estate tax is based on the taxable estate and applicable transfer-tax rules, not simply cash in probate. Include assets under gross-estate inclusion rules; evaluate marital and charitable deductions, debts, expenses, prior gifts and portability. Filing may be useful even when no tax is due to elect portability.","Generally due 9 months after date of death. An extension of time to file may be requested; an extension to file does not automatically extend time to pay. For decedents dying in 2026, the basic exclusion amount is $15,000,000 (before considering DSUE and other adjustments). Check current Form 706 instructions for filing thresholds and portability rules.","A decedent's gross estate is $14 million, with $1 million of allowable deductions and no adjusted taxable gifts. The taxable estate is not simply $14 million; deductions and the applicable exclusion determine whether federal estate tax is due and whether filing is required.","Do not confuse estate tax (Form 706, transfer from decedent) with income tax on the decedent's final Form 1040 or income tax returns for the estate (Form 1041). Portability requires a timely, complete return unless relief applies.","estate tax;gross estate;portability;basic exclusion;executor;Form 1041","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-706",["Estate tax","Transfer taxes"]),
form("990","Return of Organization Exempt From Income Tax","Exempt organizations","Annual exempt-organization information return","Provides the IRS and public with information about a tax-exempt organization's mission, governance, finances, activities, compensation, transactions and compliance. It is generally an information return rather than a tax computation for ordinary exempt-purpose income.","Most tax-exempt organizations recognized under section 501(a) generally file Form 990 or an eligible variant, unless specifically excepted. Private foundations generally file Form 990-PF. Churches and certain related organizations are generally excepted from annual Form 990-series filing.","The organization reports financial and governance information, schedules and disclosures. Unrelated business taxable income may require Form 990-T and payment of unrelated business income tax. Public inspection rules apply to many filed returns.","Form 990 is not the same as an income tax return for a taxable corporation. Exempt status does not make all income tax-free: unrelated business income, political campaign intervention and private inurement/excess benefit issues can jeopardize compliance or trigger tax.","Generally due the 15th day of the 5th month after the organization's accounting period ends. A 6-month extension is generally available using Form 8868. Eligible smaller organizations may file Form 990-EZ or 990-N instead.","An exempt organization with gross receipts of $350,000 and total assets of $700,000 generally exceeds both common Form 990-EZ size ceilings and would generally file the full Form 990, absent a special exception.","Identify the correct annual return: 990-N e-Postcard, 990-EZ, full 990, or 990-PF. The 990-EZ ceilings are generally gross receipts under $200,000 AND assets under $500,000; eligibility and exceptions must be checked.","tax-exempt;501(c)(3);public inspection;990-EZ;990-N;990-T;unrelated business income","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-990",["Exempt organizations","Information return"]),
form("2848","Power of Attorney and Declaration of Representative","Representation","Authorizes representation before the IRS","Authorizes an eligible individual to represent a taxpayer before the IRS for specified tax matters, periods and acts. It can permit the representative to receive confidential tax information and perform authorized representation acts.","A taxpayer or authorized entity representative completes and signs Form 2848 and names an eligible representative, such as an attorney, CPA or enrolled agent, within the scope of practice rules. Other eligible categories may apply in limited circumstances.","After proper execution and IRS processing, the representative may act for the taxpayer on the matters and periods listed, subject to the authority granted and IRS practice rules. The form is generally submitted to the IRS using approved submission methods.","The authorization is limited by the tax matters, periods and acts specified. A power of attorney does not transfer the taxpayer's underlying tax liability or make the representative the taxpayer. Representatives must comply with Circular 230 and conflict/confidentiality rules.","No annual filing deadline. It is effective for the tax matters and periods accepted by the IRS until revoked, withdrawn, or otherwise terminated under IRS rules. Use the current revision and instructions.","A taxpayer appoints an enrolled agent for 2024 individual income tax examination matters. The Form 2848 should identify the tax form, years and representative accurately; it does not automatically authorize representation for every future tax period.","2848 = authority to represent. Compare with Form 8821, which generally authorizes inspection/receipt of confidential tax information but does not grant representation authority.","power of attorney;representative;enrolled agent;Circular 230;tax periods;authorization","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-2848",["Representation","EA practice"]),
form("8821","Tax Information Authorization","Representation","Authorizes disclosure of tax information","Authorizes a designated person or organization to inspect and/or receive confidential tax information for specified tax types and periods. It does not authorize the designee to represent the taxpayer before the IRS.","A taxpayer or authorized person completes Form 8821 to permit a designee, such as a lender, family member or tax professional, to receive or inspect specified tax information.","Once accepted, the IRS may disclose the specified tax information to the designee. The designee cannot advocate, negotiate, sign agreements or otherwise represent the taxpayer solely by virtue of Form 8821.","The authorization is limited to the tax matters, forms and periods listed. Use Form 2848 when representation authority is needed. Disclosure authorization is not the same as a power of attorney.","No annual due date. It remains effective according to IRS processing and revocation/termination rules for the listed matters and periods. Use current instructions and submission procedures.","A mortgage lender needs transcripts for a taxpayer's 2024 and 2025 returns. Form 8821 may authorize access to those specified records, but the lender cannot negotiate an audit settlement on the taxpayer's behalf.","8821 = information access only; 2848 = representation authority. This distinction is a frequent EA exam concept.","tax information authorization;disclosure;transcripts;Form 2848;no representation authority","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8821",["Representation","EA practice"])
];

export const FLASHCARDS_PART3 = [
{id:"fc37",category:"Business entities",front:"What is the basic federal income-tax treatment of a partnership?",back:"Generally pass-through: Form 1065 reports items and each partner receives a K-1; partners generally report their shares whether or not distributed."},
{id:"fc38",category:"Business entities",front:"Does a partner pay tax only on cash distributions?",back:"No. A partner generally reports allocated taxable income even if the partnership retains the cash."},
{id:"fc39",category:"Business entities",front:"What is the major tax distinction between C corporations and S corporations?",back:"A C corporation generally pays entity-level federal income tax; an S corporation generally passes income and loss items through to shareholders, subject to limitations."},
{id:"fc40",category:"Business entities",front:"What is the calendar-year due date pattern for Forms 1065 and 1120-S?",back:"Generally March 15 (the 15th day of the third month after year-end). Form 1120 is generally due April 15 for a calendar-year corporation."},
{id:"fc41",category:"Business entities",front:"Why is reasonable compensation important for an S corporation shareholder-employee?",back:"The corporation generally must pay reasonable W-2 wages for services before treating remaining amounts as shareholder distributions; distributions cannot be used to avoid employment taxes."},
{id:"fc42",category:"Payroll",front:"Who pays FUTA tax?",back:"Generally the employer. FUTA is not withheld from employee wages; Form 940 is the annual FUTA return."},
{id:"fc43",category:"Payroll",front:"What does Form 941 report?",back:"Quarterly federal income tax withholding and Social Security/Medicare payroll tax liabilities, reconciled with deposits."},
{id:"fc44",category:"Payroll",front:"Can every small employer choose Form 944 instead of Form 941?",back:"No. The employer generally must be notified by the IRS to file Form 944; the low expected liability criterion alone is not enough."},
{id:"fc45",category:"Transfer taxes",front:"Who generally files Form 709?",back:"The donor, when gift-tax filing rules require it. A reportable taxable gift does not necessarily mean gift tax is currently payable."},
{id:"fc46",category:"Transfer taxes",front:"When is Form 706 generally due?",back:"Nine months after the decedent's date of death. A filing can be important to elect portability even if no estate tax is due."},
{id:"fc47",category:"Exempt organizations",front:"Does tax-exempt status mean an organization never files a return or pays tax?",back:"No. Many exempt organizations file an annual 990-series return, and unrelated business taxable income may be subject to tax on Form 990-T."},
{id:"fc48",category:"Representation",front:"What is the core difference between Forms 2848 and 8821?",back:"Form 2848 grants representation authority for specified matters/periods. Form 8821 authorizes access to tax information only; it does not grant representation authority."}
];

