export const FORMS_PART4 = [
form("4562","Depreciation and Amortization","Business deductions","Depreciation, Section 179 and listed property","Calculates depreciation and amortization deductions, Section 179 expensing elections, special depreciation allowances and specified listed-property information.","Taxpayers claiming depreciation for property placed in service during the year, a Section 179 deduction or carryover, or depreciation on listed property such as vehicles, subject to exceptions in the instructions.","Asset cost and business-use records feed Form 4562. The deduction generally flows to the relevant business schedule or entity return; basis is reduced by depreciation allowed or allowable.","Choose the correct recovery system and convention. Section 179, special depreciation allowance and regular MACRS depreciation have different eligibility and ordering rules. Business-use percentage, listed-property substantiation and recapture must be monitored.","For 2026, the maximum Section 179 expense deduction is $2,560,000, phased down when qualifying property placed in service exceeds $4,090,000. The 2026 SUV cap is $32,000. Certain qualified property acquired after January 19, 2025 may qualify for 100% special depreciation; confirm acquisition, binding-contract and property eligibility rules.","A business places $100,000 of eligible equipment in service in 2026. Depending on eligibility, elections, taxable-income limits and other facts, Section 179 and/or special depreciation may allow a substantial first-year deduction. The taxpayer must still track adjusted basis and future disposition consequences.","Form 4562 is not just a depreciation worksheet: test Section 179 eligibility, bonus depreciation, MACRS class life, listed-property use and recapture. Do not assume every asset qualifies for immediate expensing.","depreciation;MACRS;Section 179;bonus depreciation;listed property;amortization","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-4562",["Depreciation","Business"]),
form("4797","Sales of Business Property","Business dispositions","Business asset sales and depreciation recapture","Reports gains and losses from sales, exchanges and involuntary conversions of business property, dispositions of certain noncapital assets and depreciation recapture under provisions such as Sections 1245 and 1250.","Individuals, partnerships, S corporations and other filers may have Form 4797 reporting obligations; partnerships and S corporations generally pass certain disposition details to owners on Schedule K-1 rather than each owner treating the entity's asset sale as their own direct sale.","The form classifies the disposition and computes ordinary gain/loss, Section 1231 gain/loss and depreciation recapture. Amounts flow to Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule D or an entity return as directed by the instructions.","Section 1245 recapture generally converts gain to ordinary income to the extent of depreciation or amortization previously allowed or allowable. Section 1231 netting and the five-year lookback can change the character of net gains. Inventory and capital assets generally follow different reporting paths.","No single dollar threshold. Reporting depends on a reportable business-property disposition, involuntary conversion or recapture event. Keep acquisition cost, improvements, depreciation history, sale proceeds and selling costs.","Equipment with adjusted basis of $20,000 is sold for $35,000 after $60,000 of depreciation. The $15,000 gain may be ordinary income under Section 1245 recapture, limited to prior depreciation and subject to detailed basis and transaction rules.","Do not automatically report every asset sale on Schedule D. Identify capital asset vs Section 1231 property vs Section 1245/1250 recapture; the character of gain matters.","Section 1231;Section 1245;Section 1250;depreciation recapture;business asset sale","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-4797",["Asset dispositions","Business"]),
form("6251","Alternative Minimum Tax—Individuals","Individual tax computations","Individual alternative minimum tax","Computes alternative minimum taxable income (AMTI), the alternative minimum tax exemption and tentative minimum tax for individuals. The taxpayer compares tentative minimum tax with regular tax to determine whether AMT is due.","Individuals who have adjustments or preference items or whose income and deductions may trigger AMT. Form 6251 is generally completed when required by the Form 1040 instructions or applicable schedules.","Tax preference items and adjustments modify regular taxable income to produce AMTI. The exemption and AMT rates are applied; tentative minimum tax is compared with regular tax, with the result flowing to Form 1040.","AMT is a parallel tax system, not an extra flat surcharge on all taxpayers. Common adjustments include state and local tax deductions, incentive stock option exercise spread and certain depreciation differences. AMT foreign tax credit and other special rules may apply.","For 2026, the AMT exemption is $140,200 for joint filers/surviving spouses, $90,100 for unmarried individuals other than surviving spouses, and $70,100 for married filing separately. Phaseout begins at $1,000,000 for joint filers and $500,000 for unmarried/MFS filers; estate/trust rules differ.","A taxpayer has regular tax of $28,000 and tentative minimum tax of $31,500. Subject to the Form 6251 comparison and credits, the tentative AMT excess is $3,500.","Calculate AMTI first, then exemption and tentative minimum tax. Do not confuse AMT exemption with a deduction from regular taxable income; watch ISO exercises and SALT addbacks.","AMT;AMTI;alternative minimum tax;ISO;SALT addback;exemption","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-6251",["Alternative minimum tax","Individuals"]),
form("8995","Qualified Business Income Deduction Simplified Computation","Business deductions","Simplified Section 199A QBI deduction","Computes the qualified business income deduction for eligible taxpayers whose taxable income is at or below the applicable threshold and who meet the simplified-form criteria.","Eligible individuals, trusts and estates with qualified business income from eligible sole proprietorships, partnerships or S corporations, subject to the Section 199A rules. C corporations do not claim the individual QBI deduction.","The qualified business income, qualified REIT dividends and qualified publicly traded partnership income are entered on Form 8995. The resulting deduction generally flows to the qualified business income deduction line on Form 1040 or the applicable trust/estate return.","The general deduction is 20% of qualified business income, subject to the taxable-income limitation and exclusions/adjustments. W-2 wage/property limitations and specified service trade or business restrictions can apply above the threshold. QBI is not simply gross receipts or cash profit.","For 2026, the threshold is $201,750 for most non-joint filers and $403,500 for joint filers; married filing separately threshold is $201,775. Form 8995 is generally used when taxable income before the QBI deduction is at or below the threshold and other eligibility conditions are met.","A sole proprietor has $80,000 of qualified business income and sufficient taxable income. A preliminary 20% QBI component is $16,000, but the overall taxable-income limitation and other rules must still be applied.","Check taxable income before the QBI deduction first. Do not use Form 8995-A automatically when the simplified form applies; confirm specified service business and wage/property rules.","Section 199A;QBI;20 percent deduction;REIT dividends;PTP","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8995",["QBI deduction","Individuals"]),
form("8995-A","Qualified Business Income Deduction","Business deductions","Detailed Section 199A computation","Computes the qualified business income deduction when the taxpayer's income or business facts require the more detailed Section 199A calculations, including wage/property limitations, specified service business phaseouts and aggregation rules.","Individuals, trusts and estates with eligible qualified business income whose taxable income or business characteristics require the detailed calculation. Partnerships and S corporations provide information to owners; they do not claim the individual deduction for owners.","The taxpayer completes the applicable schedules and worksheets for each trade or business, applies wage and qualified-property limitations and any specified-service phaseout, then carries the allowable deduction to the individual or fiduciary return.","Above the threshold, the QBI component can be limited by the greater of 50% of W-2 wages or 25% of W-2 wages plus 2.5% of qualified property basis, with a phase-in range. Specified service trade or business treatment can reduce or eliminate QBI eligibility as taxable income rises. Aggregation has detailed consistency rules.","For 2026, threshold amounts are $201,750 for most non-joint filers, $201,775 for MFS, and $403,500 for MFJ; phase-in ranges extend to $276,750, $276,775 and $553,500 respectively. The phase-in range width is generally $75,000 or $150,000 for joint filers.","A joint filer with taxable income before QBI deduction of $500,000 is within the 2026 QBI phase-in range. The deduction may be subject to wage/property limits and specified-service rules; it is not automatically 20% of business profit.","Form 8995-A is the detailed pathway. Test SSTB status, taxable-income threshold, W-2 wages, qualified property and aggregation before calculating the final deduction.","QBI;Section 199A;W-2 wage limitation;qualified property;SSTB;phase-in range","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8995-a",["QBI deduction","Individuals"]),
form("1116","Foreign Tax Credit—Individuals","International taxation","Individual foreign tax credit limitation","Computes the foreign tax credit for eligible foreign income taxes paid or accrued by an individual, estate or trust, subject to category-specific limitations and sourcing rules.","Generally individuals, estates and trusts claiming credit for qualifying foreign income, war profits or excess profits taxes, unless an exception permits a limited election to claim certain taxes directly on Schedule 3 without Form 1116.","Foreign-source taxable income is separated into applicable categories, deductions are allocated, and the credit limitation is computed. The allowable credit generally flows to Schedule 3 and then Form 1040; unused foreign tax credits may be carried under statutory rules.","The credit is generally limited to the U.S. tax attributable to foreign-source taxable income in each separate category. Foreign tax paid on income not eligible for credit, or tax that is not an income tax under U.S. rules, may not qualify. A deduction for foreign taxes may be an alternative but generally cannot be claimed for the same taxes as a credit.","No single income threshold. The optional exception from Form 1116 is narrow and generally requires only passive-category foreign income reported on qualified payee statements, foreign tax within the statutory limit and all other election conditions.","A taxpayer has $10,000 qualifying foreign-source taxable income and $100,000 worldwide taxable income. The foreign-source fraction helps determine the U.S. tax limitation; the credit is not automatically equal to all foreign tax paid.","Foreign tax credit is limited by U.S. tax on foreign-source income. Use separate Form 1116 calculations for each applicable income category and distinguish credit from deduction.","foreign tax credit;foreign-source income;passive category;general category;carryover","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1116",["Foreign tax credit","International"]),
form("2555","Foreign Earned Income","International taxation","Foreign earned income and housing exclusions","Claims the foreign earned income exclusion and, when eligible, the foreign housing exclusion or deduction for qualifying individuals with a foreign tax home who meet the bona fide residence or physical presence test.","A U.S. citizen or qualifying U.S. resident alien whose tax home is in a foreign country and who meets either the bona fide residence test or physical presence test. U.S. government employee income is generally not eligible for these exclusions.","The taxpayer calculates qualifying foreign earned income, qualifying period, housing amount and exclusions on Form 2555. The exclusion is reported on the individual return as instructed; tax-rate stacking and deduction allocation rules may still affect the final tax.","A foreign address or foreign employer alone does not qualify a taxpayer. The tax-home test and one of the residence/presence tests must be met. The exclusion applies to earned income for personal services performed abroad, not generally to interest, dividends, capital gains or pensions.","For 2026, the maximum foreign earned income exclusion is $132,900, subject to qualification and proration for a qualifying period shorter than the full year. Housing limits vary by location and applicable rules.","A qualifying taxpayer earns $150,000 of foreign wages during a full 2026 qualifying year. The maximum exclusion is $132,900; the remaining amount and any housing exclusion are determined under the detailed rules and worksheet.","Foreign earned income exclusion is not automatic for anyone working abroad. Confirm tax home, bona fide residence/physical presence, qualifying days, source of services and foreign housing expenses.","foreign earned income;FEIE;tax home;physical presence;bonafide residence;housing exclusion","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-2555",["Foreign earned income","International"]),
form("5471","Information Return of U.S. Persons With Respect to Certain Foreign Corporations","International taxation","Foreign corporation ownership reporting","Reports ownership, control, financial information and specified transactions involving certain foreign corporations. It supports U.S. international tax reporting regimes, including controlled foreign corporation and other foreign corporation rules.","Certain U.S. persons who are officers, directors or shareholders of specified foreign corporations and meet one or more filing categories under Sections 6038 and 6046. Filing category and schedules depend on ownership, control and transactions.","The filer completes the applicable Form 5471 schedules and attaches the form to the relevant U.S. income tax, partnership or exempt-organization return. The information may support Subpart F, GILTI/Section 951A, foreign tax credit and other calculations.","Form 5471 is an information return, but failure to file can trigger substantial penalties and continuation penalties. A separate form is generally required for each foreign corporation; filing categories and exceptions must be analyzed carefully.","No single general dollar threshold. Filing depends on U.S. person status, ownership percentages, control, acquisitions/dispositions, officer/director status and the applicable filer category. Attach by the due date, including extensions, of the related return.","A U.S. individual acquires a controlling interest in a foreign corporation. The individual must analyze the Form 5471 filer categories and schedules, even if the foreign company paid no dividend during the year.","Foreign corporation ownership can trigger reporting without cash distributions. Determine filer category and CFC status, then assess Subpart F, GILTI/Section 951A and foreign tax credit consequences.","CFC;foreign corporation;Form 5471;Subpart F;GILTI;ownership reporting","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-5471",["Foreign corporations","International"]),
form("1041","U.S. Income Tax Return for Estates and Trusts","Fiduciary taxation","Income tax return for estates and trusts","Reports income, deductions, gains, losses, credits and distributions of a domestic estate or trust. It computes fiduciary taxable income and the distribution deduction, and identifies amounts passed through to beneficiaries.","A domestic estate generally files if it has gross income of $600 or more, a nonresident-alien beneficiary, or other specified filing triggers. A domestic trust generally files if it has any taxable income, gross income of $600 or more, or a nonresident-alien beneficiary, subject to exceptions and grantor-trust rules.","The fiduciary files Form 1041 and prepares Schedule K-1 (1041) for beneficiaries. Income retained by the estate/trust may be taxed at fiduciary level; distributable net income (DNI) rules determine the character and amount passed to beneficiaries.","Trusts and estates are separate taxpayers for federal income-tax purposes unless grantor-trust rules treat income as owned by another person. Simple and complex trusts have different distribution requirements. DNI, distributable income deduction, capital gains allocation and compressed fiduciary tax brackets are key.","For calendar-year tax years, Form 1041 is generally due April 15 following the close of the tax year. A domestic estate or trust filing threshold often includes $600 gross income, but taxable income, nonresident-alien beneficiaries and other triggers can require filing even below that amount. Verify current-year instructions.","A trust receives $20,000 interest and dividends and distributes $15,000 of distributable net income to beneficiaries. The fiduciary and beneficiaries determine the taxable portions under DNI and K-1 rules; a cash distribution is not automatically all taxable income.","Distinguish fiduciary accounting income, taxable income and DNI. Form 1041 can tax retained income at the trust/estate level and pass taxable items to beneficiaries via K-1.","fiduciary;estate;trust;DNI;simple trust;complex trust;Schedule K-1 1041","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1041",["Trusts and estates","Fiduciary"]),
form("Schedule K-1 (1041)","Beneficiary's Share of Income, Deductions, Credits, etc.","Fiduciary taxation","Beneficiary tax allocation","Reports a beneficiary's share of an estate's or trust's income, deductions, credits and other tax items. It provides the tax character and amounts the beneficiary may need to report on their own return.","The estate or trust fiduciary prepares a separate Schedule K-1 for each beneficiary and files the copies with Form 1041 as required. Each beneficiary receives their K-1.","The beneficiary reports the items on the appropriate Form 1040 lines and schedules, preserving their character—for example, interest as interest and qualified dividends as dividends. K-1 statements may contain additional allocation information.","The beneficiary generally includes amounts based on DNI and the applicable trust/estate distribution rules, not simply the cash received. Grantor trusts generally report owner income under grantor-trust procedures rather than using K-1 to report the grantor's items.","For calendar-year estates and trusts, the K-1 is generally furnished by the Form 1041 due date, generally April 15 following year-end, subject to extensions and applicable rules.","A beneficiary receives $4,000 taxable interest and $1,000 qualified dividends on a K-1. These items retain their character and are not combined into a generic $5,000 distribution line.","Read each box and code carefully; preserve income character. A K-1 is a tax allocation statement, not a bank statement showing the cash distribution.","beneficiary;trust K-1;estate K-1;DNI;income character;fiduciary","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-k-1-form-1041",["Trusts and estates","K-1"]),
form("8865","Return of U.S. Persons With Respect to Certain Foreign Partnerships","International taxation","Foreign partnership reporting","Reports specified ownership, control, transfers and changes in interests involving foreign partnerships under Sections 6038, 6038B and 6046A.","U.S. persons who meet one or more categories of filer, including certain controlling partners, 10% partners in specified controlled foreign partnerships, property transferors and persons acquiring or disposing of reportable partnership interests.","The filer attaches Form 8865 and required schedules to the related U.S. income tax, partnership or exempt-organization return. Schedules can report ownership, income, balance sheet, related-party transactions, transfers and partner information.","Form 8865 is an international information return, not simply the domestic Form 1065 filed by every foreign partnership. Filing categories and exceptions determine which schedules apply. Penalties can apply for failure to file or provide required information.","No single universal dollar threshold. Ownership percentages, control, transfers and acquisitions/dispositions determine filing categories. Attach to the related return by its due date, including extensions.","A U.S. person owns more than 50% of a foreign partnership. The person generally analyzes Category 1 filing status and required schedules, even if the partnership did not distribute cash.","Do not confuse Form 8865 with Form 1065. The former is a U.S. person's international information return regarding certain foreign partnerships.","foreign partnership;Form 8865;controlled partnership;international reporting;Section 6038B","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8865",["Foreign partnerships","International"]),
form("8938","Statement of Specified Foreign Financial Assets","International taxation","FATCA individual asset disclosure","Reports specified foreign financial assets when the taxpayer meets applicable filing thresholds. It is attached to the income tax return and provides information about reportable foreign financial accounts and other specified assets.","Specified individuals and certain domestic entities that meet the applicable asset thresholds. Specified individuals generally include U.S. citizens, resident aliens and certain nonresident aliens who elect or qualify for specified treatment.","Form 8938 is attached to Form 1040 or other applicable income tax return. It is distinct from the FBAR (FinCEN Form 114), which is filed separately with FinCEN and has different definitions, thresholds and deadlines.","A foreign account can be reportable under both Form 8938 and FBAR, one, or neither. Form 8938 does not replace other required reporting such as FBAR, Form 5471, Form 8865 or Form 8621. Income from assets is still reported on the tax return even when Form 8938 is not required.","For taxpayers living in the United States, common thresholds are more than $50,000 on the last day or more than $75,000 at any time for unmarried/MFS, and more than $100,000/$150,000 for joint filers. Higher thresholds apply to qualifying taxpayers living abroad: $200,000/$300,000 single and $400,000/$600,000 joint. Special entity rules apply.","A U.S.-resident unmarried taxpayer holds specified foreign assets worth $80,000 at year-end. The taxpayer exceeds the common $50,000 year-end threshold and generally must evaluate Form 8938 filing, even if an FBAR analysis is separate.","Form 8938 = IRS tax-return attachment; FBAR = FinCEN report. Test both independently and do not assume one filing satisfies the other.","FATCA;foreign financial assets;Form 8938;FBAR;FinCEN;living abroad thresholds","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8938",["Foreign assets","International"]),
form("8621","Information Return by a Shareholder of a Passive Foreign Investment Company or Qualified Electing Fund","International taxation","PFIC shareholder reporting","Reports specified information and elections relating to ownership of a passive foreign investment company (PFIC), including distributions, dispositions, qualified electing fund treatment and mark-to-market elections.","Generally U.S. persons who are direct or indirect shareholders of a PFIC and meet one of the reporting circumstances, subject to exceptions. Separate Forms 8621 may be required for each PFIC.","Form 8621 is generally attached to the shareholder's income tax return. PFIC income, distributions and dispositions may be subject to special tax and interest computations; QEF and mark-to-market elections can change the treatment.","A foreign corporation can be a PFIC if it meets either the 75% passive-income test or the 50% passive-asset test, subject to statutory definitions and exceptions. PFIC treatment is separate from CFC rules, though overlap can occur.","No single dollar threshold applies to all filing circumstances. Filing can be required for certain distributions, gain on disposition, elections, QEF/mark-to-market reporting or annual reporting under Section 1298(f), subject to exceptions.","A U.S. taxpayer owns shares in a foreign investment company whose passive income is at least 75% of gross income. The company may meet the PFIC income test; the shareholder should analyze Form 8621 reporting and tax treatment.","Foreign mutual funds may be PFICs. Do not assume ordinary foreign dividend/capital-gain treatment; identify PFIC status and consider elections and annual filing obligations.","PFIC;QEF;mark-to-market;Section 1291;foreign mutual fund;Form 8621","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8621",["PFIC","International"])
];

export const FLASHCARDS_PART4 = [
{id:"fc49",category:"Business deductions",front:"What is Form 4562 used for?",back:"Depreciation and amortization, Section 179 expensing elections, special depreciation allowance, and specified listed-property reporting."},
{id:"fc50",category:"Business dispositions",front:"What does Section 1245 depreciation recapture generally do?",back:"It generally treats gain as ordinary income to the extent of depreciation or amortization previously allowed or allowable."},
{id:"fc51",category:"Alternative minimum tax",front:"What is the purpose of Form 6251?",back:"It computes AMTI, applies the AMT exemption and rates, and determines tentative minimum tax for comparison with regular tax."},
{id:"fc52",category:"QBI deduction",front:"When is Form 8995 generally used instead of 8995-A?",back:"When the taxpayer qualifies for the simplified Section 199A calculation, generally with taxable income before the QBI deduction at or below the applicable threshold and no disqualifying complexity."},
{id:"fc53",category:"QBI deduction",front:"What limitation can restrict the QBI component above the Section 199A threshold?",back:"Generally the greater of 50% of W-2 wages or 25% of W-2 wages plus 2.5% of qualified property basis, with a phase-in range and other rules."},
{id:"fc54",category:"International taxation",front:"How is the foreign tax credit on Form 1116 generally limited?",back:"It is generally limited to the U.S. tax attributable to foreign-source taxable income in each separate income category."},
{id:"fc55",category:"International taxation",front:"What tests generally qualify a taxpayer for Form 2555?",back:"The taxpayer needs a foreign tax home and must meet either the bona fide residence test or physical presence test."},
{id:"fc56",category:"International taxation",front:"What does Form 5471 report?",back:"Certain U.S. persons' ownership, control and specified information about certain foreign corporations; filing categories and schedules depend on facts."},
{id:"fc57",category:"Fiduciary taxation",front:"What is distributable net income (DNI) used for?",back:"DNI generally limits the amount of taxable income carried out to beneficiaries and helps preserve the character of distributed income."},
{id:"fc58",category:"International taxation",front:"Does Form 8938 replace the FBAR?",back:"No. Form 8938 is filed with the tax return; FBAR (FinCEN Form 114) is filed separately. Analyze each independently."},
{id:"fc59",category:"International taxation",front:"What does Form 8865 generally cover?",back:"Specified U.S. persons' reporting about certain foreign partnerships, including control, ownership, transfers and changes in interests."},
{id:"fc60",category:"International taxation",front:"What is a PFIC, and which form may report it?",back:"A Passive Foreign Investment Company generally meets a passive-income or passive-asset test. U.S. shareholders may have Form 8621 filing obligations."}
];

