// Part 5 — IRS procedures, examinations, appeals, collections and taxpayer remedies.
export const FORMS_PART5 = [
form("12153","Request for a Collection Due Process or Equivalent Hearing","IRS procedure","Request a CDP or equivalent hearing","Requests an administrative hearing with the IRS Independent Office of Appeals after certain lien or levy notices. The hearing can address collection alternatives and, in limited circumstances, the underlying tax liability.","Taxpayers who receive a qualifying Notice of Federal Tax Lien filing or Final Notice of Intent to Levy that provides CDP rights; an equivalent-hearing request may be available after the CDP deadline, but rights differ.","The taxpayer identifies the notice, tax periods, proposed collection action and issues for Appeals. A timely CDP request generally preserves the right to seek Tax Court review of the CDP determination.","A timely CDP request is generally due within 30 days of the date of the qualifying notice. An equivalent hearing is generally requested within the one-year period described in the notice and instructions, but it does not carry the same Tax Court review rights.","Generally 30 days from the notice date for a timely CDP hearing; read the specific notice because the deadline and appeal rights are critical.","A taxpayer receives a Final Notice of Intent to Levy dated June 1. A properly submitted Form 12153 within the notice's 30-day period can request a CDP hearing and raise collection alternatives.","Distinguish CDP from CAP. CDP generally offers Tax Court review if timely; CAP is faster in some collection disputes but generally does not provide Tax Court review.","CDP;collection due process;levy;federal tax lien;Appeals;12153","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-12153",["Collections","Appeals"]),
form("9465","Installment Agreement Request","IRS procedure","Request monthly payments","Requests an installment agreement to pay a federal tax liability over time rather than in one immediate payment.","Individuals and certain businesses that cannot pay their tax debt in full by the due date and meet the applicable eligibility and compliance requirements.","The taxpayer proposes payment terms; the IRS evaluates the balance, filing compliance and financial facts. Interest and applicable penalties generally continue until the balance is paid.","An installment agreement is not forgiveness of tax. A pending or approved agreement generally restricts certain levy actions, subject to statutory exceptions and default rules. Direct debit or payroll deduction may be required for some arrangements.","Eligibility, streamlined balance limits, fees and terms vary by taxpayer type and current IRS procedures. Check the current online payment agreement tool and Form 9465 instructions.","A taxpayer owes $8,000 and can pay $400 monthly. The taxpayer requests an installment agreement; the debt continues to accrue applicable interest and penalties while being paid.","An extension to file does not extend payment time. An installment agreement addresses collection, not the correctness of the assessment.","installment agreement;monthly payments;payment plan;9465","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-9465",["Collections","Payment"]),
form("656","Offer in Compromise","IRS procedure","Settle qualifying tax debt","Submits an offer to resolve an eligible tax liability for less than the full amount owed when statutory and administrative criteria are satisfied.","Taxpayers who may qualify under doubt as to collectibility, doubt as to liability, or effective tax administration grounds. Required returns and current tax obligations generally must be addressed before an offer can be considered.","Form 656 states the offer and grounds; financial disclosure forms and supporting documentation are generally required for doubt-as-to-collectibility offers. The IRS evaluates reasonable collection potential and applicable standards.","An offer is not automatically accepted because a taxpayer cannot pay immediately. The IRS considers assets, income, allowable expenses, future earning potential and the legal basis for the liability. A rejected offer may generally be appealed within 30 days.","No single universal income or debt threshold guarantees acceptance. The amount offered and eligibility depend on the taxpayer's facts, financial disclosure and offer basis.","A taxpayer has a $40,000 assessed balance but limited assets and income. The IRS evaluates realizable equity and future disposable income before deciding whether a collectibility-based offer is acceptable.","OIC is not the same as an installment agreement or currently-not-collectible status. Analyze all three options and the offer's legal basis.","offer in compromise;OIC;doubt as to collectibility;656","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-656",["Collections","Resolution"]),
form("433-A","Collection Information Statement for Wage Earners and Self-Employed Individuals","IRS procedure","Individual financial disclosure","Collects detailed information about an individual's income, expenses, assets, liabilities and financial circumstances for collection analysis.","Individuals, wage earners and self-employed taxpayers when the IRS requests a financial statement or when applying for certain collection alternatives.","The IRS uses the completed statement and supporting records to evaluate ability to pay, installment agreement terms, offer in compromise or currently-not-collectible status.","Report complete and accurate information. Allowable expense standards and documentation rules may affect the amount the IRS considers available for collection.","There is no single filing threshold; use when required by the collection process or the applicable offer/payment arrangement instructions.","A self-employed taxpayer provides bank balances, vehicle equity, monthly business income and allowable living expenses so the IRS can evaluate a collection alternative.","Do not confuse Form 433-A (individual financial disclosure) with Form 433-B (business financial disclosure).","433-A;collection information statement;financial disclosure;individual","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-433-a",["Collections","Financial disclosure"]),
form("433-B","Collection Information Statement for Businesses","IRS procedure","Business financial disclosure","Collects financial information about a business, including assets, liabilities, income, expenses and accounts receivable, to evaluate collection options.","Businesses when the IRS requests financial information for collection analysis, including certain installment agreement, offer in compromise or hardship matters.","The business reports financial facts and supporting records. The IRS evaluates business cash flow, asset equity and ability to pay when determining collection terms.","Information must be complete and current. The IRS may request bank statements, accounts receivable aging, asset records and other substantiation.","No universal dollar threshold; use when required by the relevant IRS collection procedure.","A corporation owing payroll taxes submits Form 433-B with cash balances, equipment equity, receivables and operating expenses for collection review.","Form 433-B concerns business finances; an owner's personal financial information may be separately requested.","433-B;business collection statement;business assets;cash flow","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-433-b",["Collections","Financial disclosure"]),
form("433-F","Collection Information Statement","IRS procedure","Simplified financial disclosure","A collection information statement used to gather financial information, often in individual collection cases, to help determine ability to pay.","Individuals who are asked by the IRS to provide a financial statement in connection with collection activity.","The taxpayer reports income, expenses, bank accounts, investments, assets and liabilities. The IRS uses the information to assess payment capacity and collection alternatives.","Complete all requested fields accurately and provide supporting documentation when requested. The form is not itself a tax return or an offer in compromise.","Use when requested or when the applicable IRS process directs the taxpayer to submit it; requirements vary by collection case.","An individual with a past-due balance completes Form 433-F to disclose monthly take-home pay, rent, utilities, vehicle loan and bank accounts.","Know the purpose of 433-F versus the more detailed 433-A and business-focused 433-B.","433-F;collection information statement;individual assets;expenses","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-433-f",["Collections","Financial disclosure"]),
form("843","Claim for Refund and Request for Abatement","IRS procedure","Request certain refunds or penalty abatements","Requests a refund or abatement of certain taxes, penalties, additions to tax or interest where Form 843 is the appropriate vehicle.","Taxpayers seeking relief for eligible assessed penalties or other amounts covered by the form instructions. It is not a universal substitute for an amended income tax return.","The claimant identifies the tax, period, amount, grounds and supporting facts. The IRS reviews the legal basis and evidence; other claim procedures may apply to income tax corrections.","Penalty relief may be available through statutory exceptions, administrative waiver such as first-time abatement where eligible, or reasonable cause. Reasonable cause is fact-specific and requires ordinary business care and prudence under the applicable standard.","Refund/abatement limitation periods depend on the type of tax and claim. Generally, refund claims are subject to the later-of-three-years-from-filing or two-years-from-payment rule, with exceptions.","A taxpayer requests abatement of a late-filing penalty and documents a qualifying event, actions taken to comply and prompt correction after the event ended.","Lack of funds alone generally is not reasonable cause for failure to pay. First-time abatement is an administrative waiver, not the same legal test as reasonable cause.","843;penalty abatement;reasonable cause;first time abate;refund claim","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-843",["Penalty relief","Refund claims"]),
form("911","Request for Taxpayer Advocate Service Assistance","IRS procedure","Request help with unresolved tax problems","Requests assistance from the independent Taxpayer Advocate Service when a taxpayer is experiencing significant hardship or an IRS problem that has not been resolved through normal channels.","Taxpayers who meet TAS case acceptance criteria, such as experiencing economic harm, facing an immediate threat of adverse action, or having an IRS problem that remains unresolved after repeated attempts.","The taxpayer describes the issue, prior IRS contacts, hardship and requested relief. TAS reviews whether it can accept the case and may work with IRS functions to resolve the problem.","TAS is independent within the IRS and does not replace ordinary appeal procedures, legal representation or statutory deadlines. Filing Form 911 does not automatically suspend deadlines.","No fixed income threshold applies to every case. Acceptance depends on TAS criteria and the facts of the taxpayer's problem.","A taxpayer faces imminent utility shutoff because an IRS refund freeze has caused severe hardship and repeated IRS contacts have not resolved the issue; Form 911 may request TAS assistance.","TAS help is generally free. Do not assume Form 911 automatically extends a Tax Court petition or appeal deadline.","Taxpayer Advocate Service;TAS;economic hardship;911","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-911",["Taxpayer rights","Assistance"]),
form("12203","Request for Appeals Review","IRS procedure","Request review of an examination decision","Requests an Appeals review of certain proposed examination adjustments when the taxpayer disagrees and the case qualifies for the small case request procedure.","Taxpayers who receive an examination report and meet the eligibility criteria described in the form instructions; larger or more complex cases may require a formal written protest.","The taxpayer identifies disputed adjustments and explains the reasons for disagreement. The case is referred to the IRS Independent Office of Appeals for an impartial administrative review.","Follow the response deadline in the examination letter. Some cases require a formal protest with specific declarations and supporting legal/factual analysis rather than Form 12203.","Eligibility and the amount-in-dispute criteria are set out in current IRS instructions; check the version attached to the examination letter.","An individual disagrees with a proposed $4,000 adjustment and qualifies to request Appeals review using Form 12203 within the stated response period.","A 30-day letter is an opportunity to request Appeals review; a 90-day notice of deficiency generally starts a separate Tax Court petition deadline.","12203;Appeals review;examination;30-day letter","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-12203",["Examinations","Appeals"]),
form("8857","Request for Innocent Spouse Relief","IRS procedure","Request relief from joint tax liability","Requests relief from joint and several liability for certain tax liabilities arising from a joint return, including innocent spouse, separation of liability or equitable relief.","A spouse or former spouse who filed a joint return and believes they should not be held responsible for all or part of an understatement or underpayment under the applicable statutory tests.","The requesting spouse submits facts about the return, knowledge, marital/economic circumstances and liability. The IRS may notify the other spouse and evaluate the statutory and equitable factors.","Relief types have different eligibility tests and deadlines. A request for equitable relief for an underpayment is generally subject to a different timing rule than relief for an understatement; use current instructions and analyze the specific relief type.","There is no simple dollar threshold. The requesting spouse must satisfy the requirements for the particular relief category.","A spouse signed a joint return but did not know and had no reason to know of a substantial omitted business income item; the facts may support analysis for innocent spouse relief.","Joint filers are generally jointly and severally liable. Divorce or separation alone does not automatically eliminate liability.","innocent spouse;separation of liability;equitable relief;joint return;8857","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8857",["Taxpayer relief","Individuals"])
];

export const FLASHCARDS_PART5 = [
{id:"fc61",category:"IRS examinations",front:"What is the basic sequence after an IRS examination proposes changes?",back:"The taxpayer reviews the report, may agree or request administrative Appeals review, and may receive a notice of deficiency (90-day letter) if the dispute is not resolved. Follow the deadline in the actual notice."},
{id:"fc62",category:"Appeals",front:"What is the key difference between a 30-day letter and a 90-day letter?",back:"A 30-day letter generally offers an administrative Appeals opportunity. A 90-day notice of deficiency generally gives the taxpayer a statutory period to petition the U.S. Tax Court before assessment, subject to the notice and special rules."},
{id:"fc63",category:"Collections",front:"What is the difference between a tax lien and a tax levy?",back:"A lien is the government's legal claim against property as security for a tax debt. A levy is the legal seizure of property or rights to property to satisfy the debt."},
{id:"fc64",category:"Collections",front:"What is a Collection Due Process hearing?",back:"An Appeals hearing available after certain lien or levy notices. A timely request is generally due within 30 days and may preserve Tax Court review rights."},
{id:"fc65",category:"Collections",front:"Does an installment agreement stop interest and penalties?",back:"No. Applicable interest and penalties generally continue until the balance is paid, even while the taxpayer makes payments under an agreement."},
{id:"fc66",category:"Collections",front:"What are the three broad Offer in Compromise grounds?",back:"Doubt as to collectibility, doubt as to liability, and effective tax administration."},
{id:"fc67",category:"Collections",front:"What does currently not collectible (CNC) status mean?",back:"The IRS temporarily delays active collection because the taxpayer cannot pay without undue hardship. The debt remains; interest and penalties generally continue and a lien may still be filed."},
{id:"fc68",category:"Statutes",front:"What is the general federal assessment limitation period?",back:"Generally three years from filing, subject to exceptions such as a substantial omission (generally six years), fraud or failure to file a valid return (no limit), and suspensions/extensions."},
{id:"fc69",category:"Statutes",front:"What is the general federal collection limitation period?",back:"Generally 10 years from the date of assessment, but certain events can suspend or extend the period. Calculate from the actual assessment and account for tolling events."},
{id:"fc70",category:"Penalty relief",front:"What is First Time Abate (FTA)?",back:"An IRS administrative waiver that may remove certain failure-to-file, failure-to-pay or failure-to-deposit penalties when eligibility and compliance-history criteria are met. It is distinct from reasonable cause."},
{id:"fc71",category:"Penalty relief",front:"What is the reasonable-cause standard for penalty relief?",back:"The taxpayer generally must show ordinary business care and prudence but was nevertheless unable to comply, evaluated under all facts and circumstances. Mere lack of funds alone generally is not enough for failure to pay."},
{id:"fc72",category:"Taxpayer rights",front:"What is the Taxpayer Advocate Service for?",back:"It helps taxpayers experiencing significant hardship or unresolved IRS problems that have not been resolved through normal channels. Form 911 can request assistance; it does not automatically extend legal deadlines."},
{id:"fc73",category:"Taxpayer rights",front:"Does a tax lien mean the IRS has seized the taxpayer's property?",back:"No. A lien is a legal claim/security interest; a levy is the actual seizure of property or rights to property."},
{id:"fc74",category:"Spousal relief",front:"Does divorce automatically release a former spouse from joint tax liability?",back:"No. Joint and several liability generally remains after divorce unless the taxpayer qualifies for statutory innocent-spouse, separation-of-liability or equitable relief."}
];

