// Part 6 — Circular 230, professional ethics, due diligence and practitioner conduct.
export const FORMS_PART6 = [
form("8867","Paid Preparer's Due Diligence Checklist","Professional responsibility","Document due diligence for specified tax benefits","A paid preparer uses Form 8867 to document required due diligence for specified tax benefits and filing status. The checklist is part of the preparer's compliance process and is generally submitted with the return when required.","Paid tax return preparers who prepare returns or claims involving the Earned Income Tax Credit, Child Tax Credit / Additional Child Tax Credit / Credit for Other Dependents, American Opportunity Tax Credit, or head-of-household filing status, as applicable under the instructions.","The preparer completes the checklist, asks follow-up questions when information appears incomplete or inconsistent, retains required records and submits the form as directed with the return.","Due diligence is not satisfied by blindly copying client answers. Preparers must meet the knowledge, recordkeeping, completion and submission requirements in the current regulations and Form 8867 instructions. Separate per-benefit requirements and exceptions apply.","The form applies when the return claims one or more covered benefits/statuses and the preparer is subject to the paid-preparer due-diligence rules. Review current Form 8867 instructions and applicable regulations for retention periods and exact requirements.","A client claims EITC and AOTC. The preparer asks about the child's residency and student enrollment, evaluates supporting facts, records the answers and completes the applicable Form 8867 sections.","Know which credits/statuses trigger Form 8867. Due diligence requires reasonable inquiry when information appears incorrect, inconsistent or incomplete.","Form 8867;paid preparer;due diligence;EITC;CTC;AOTC;head of household","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8867",["Due diligence","Preparers"]),
form("8275","Disclosure Statement","Professional responsibility","Disclose certain tax return positions","Form 8275 is used to disclose items or positions on a tax return that are not otherwise adequately disclosed, generally to help satisfy disclosure requirements and potentially avoid certain accuracy-related penalties when statutory and regulatory requirements are met.","Taxpayers and return preparers who need to disclose a tax position or item as permitted or required by the Code, regulations and instructions. Disclosure does not make an otherwise improper position lawful.","The form identifies the relevant return item, amount, legal authority and factual explanation. It is attached to the return as directed by the instructions.","Adequate disclosure may affect certain penalty defenses, but it does not eliminate tax, guarantee penalty relief or protect a position lacking reasonable basis where a higher standard applies. Some positions require Form 8275-R rather than Form 8275.","No dollar threshold applies universally. Whether disclosure is adequate depends on the specific Code provision, regulation, form instructions and nature of the position.","A taxpayer takes a return position that is not contrary to a regulation but requires disclosure under applicable rules. The preparer evaluates whether Form 8275 is appropriate and describes the facts and authority accurately.","Form 8275 is not the same as Form 8275-R. The latter is generally used for a position contrary to a regulation and carries distinct penalty and disclosure implications.","8275;disclosure statement;tax position;accuracy-related penalty;substantial authority","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8275",["Disclosure","Preparers"]),
form("14157","Complaint: Tax Return Preparer","Professional responsibility","Report suspected preparer misconduct","Form 14157 allows a taxpayer to report suspected misconduct by a tax return preparer, such as improper conduct, unauthorized changes, or other listed preparer issues.","Taxpayers who believe a tax return preparer engaged in misconduct described in the form instructions. Different procedures may apply to identity theft or unauthorized e-file issues.","The taxpayer provides preparer identification, return details, a description of the alleged conduct and supporting information. The IRS routes the complaint for appropriate review.","A complaint is an allegation for IRS review; filing Form 14157 does not itself amend the taxpayer's return, resolve a tax balance or guarantee a particular enforcement result.","No monetary threshold. Use the form when the facts match the complaint categories and follow the current instructions, including any related Form 14157-A requirements.","A preparer changes a taxpayer's bank account information without authorization. The taxpayer documents the issue and follows IRS instructions for reporting the preparer's conduct.","Distinguish a preparer misconduct complaint from a taxpayer's own return correction or identity-theft report.","14157;preparer complaint;misconduct;tax preparer","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-14157",["Complaints","Preparers"])
];

export const FLASHCARDS_PART6 = [
{id:"fc75",category:"Circular 230",front:"What is Circular 230?",back:"Treasury Department regulations (31 CFR Part 10) governing practice before the IRS. They set practitioner eligibility, duties, restrictions and disciplinary procedures."},
{id:"fc76",category:"Circular 230",front:"Which professionals generally have unlimited practice rights before the IRS?",back:"Attorneys, CPAs in good standing and enrolled agents, subject to applicable rules. Other persons may have limited practice rights in specified circumstances."},
{id:"fc77",category:"Due diligence",front:"What does Circular 230 §10.22 require?",back:"A practitioner must exercise due diligence in preparing and filing submissions, determining correctness of representations to the IRS and clients, and related professional conduct. Reasonable care is required when relying on others."},
{id:"fc78",category:"Competence",front:"What is the competence rule under Circular 230 §10.35?",back:"A practitioner must possess or obtain the necessary knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation for the matter. Competence may be achieved through appropriate study or consultation."},
{id:"fc79",category:"Conflicts",front:"When does a conflict of interest arise under Circular 230 §10.29?",back:"When representation is directly adverse to another client, or there is a significant risk that responsibilities to another client, former client, third person or personal interest will materially limit representation. Informed written consent may permit representation only if the rule's conditions are met."},
{id:"fc80",category:"Conflicts",front:"What are the two key conditions for proceeding despite a conflict under §10.29?",back:"The practitioner must reasonably believe competent and diligent representation can be provided to each affected client, and each affected client must give informed consent, confirmed in writing, with required records retained."},
{id:"fc81",category:"Written advice",front:"What is the central standard in Circular 230 §10.37 for written tax advice?",back:"Written advice must be based on reasonable factual and legal assumptions, consider relevant facts and circumstances, use reasonable efforts to identify and ascertain relevant facts, and relate the law to those facts without unreasonable reliance or unsupported conclusions."},
{id:"fc82",category:"Tax return standards",front:"Can a practitioner sign or advise a position merely because the client insists?",back:"No. Circular 230 §10.34 and other applicable standards require an appropriate basis for positions and prohibit advising or signing returns with positions that violate the governing standards. A client instruction does not excuse misconduct."},
{id:"fc83",category:"Client records",front:"What is the general rule for client records under Circular 230 §10.28?",back:"A practitioner generally must promptly return client records necessary for the taxpayer to comply with federal tax obligations when requested, subject to the regulation's limits and applicable law. A fee dispute does not authorize withholding essential client records contrary to the rule."},
{id:"fc84",category:"Fees",front:"Are contingent fees generally permitted for all IRS matters?",back:"No. Circular 230 §10.27 restricts contingent fees, with specified exceptions. Never assume a percentage-of-tax-savings fee is permissible; analyze the exact matter and regulatory exception."},
{id:"fc85",category:"Solicitation",front:"What must a practitioner avoid in advertising or solicitation under Circular 230 §10.30?",back:"False, fraudulent, coercive, misleading or deceptive statements or claims. Communications must not create unjustified expectations or misrepresent credentials, experience or results."},
{id:"fc86",category:"Supervision",front:"What does Circular 230 §10.36 require of a firm with responsibility for tax practice?",back:"A responsible practitioner must take reasonable steps to ensure the firm has adequate procedures to comply with Circular 230. A firm can face consequences when required procedures are absent or ineffective and the rule's conditions are met."},
{id:"fc87",category:"Sanctions",front:"What sanctions can the OPR impose or pursue under Circular 230?",back:"Censure, suspension, disbarment, monetary penalties and, for appraisers, disqualification, as applicable. The OPR investigates and administers practitioner discipline."},
{id:"fc88",category:"Due diligence",front:"What is the purpose of Form 8867?",back:"It documents paid-preparer due diligence for specified benefits and filing status, including EITC, CTC/ACTC/ODC, AOTC and head-of-household status as applicable. It does not replace required inquiries and records."},
{id:"fc89",category:"Disclosure",front:"Does Form 8275 make an otherwise improper tax position acceptable?",back:"No. Disclosure can affect certain penalty rules when statutory requirements are met, but it does not make an unlawful or unsupported position correct or eliminate the underlying tax."},
{id:"fc90",category:"Ethical practice",front:"A client asks you to omit cash income. What should you do?",back:"Do not knowingly prepare or submit a false return or assist evasion. Explain the reporting obligation, document relevant communications, decline the improper request and consider withdrawal or other appropriate steps consistent with law and professional duties."}
];

