// Part 8 — advanced, scenario-based EA practice questions.
// Questions emphasize analysis and return flow; verify year-specific thresholds separately.
export const QUESTIONS_PART8 = [
// Part 1 — Individuals: advanced scenarios
{id:"p1q11",part:1,topic:"Dependency and filing status",question:"A taxpayer's 19-year-old full-time college student lived with the taxpayer for 8 months and was away at school for 4 months. The student did not provide more than half of their own support. Which statement is generally correct?",choices:["The student fails the residency test because they were away 4 months","The temporary absence for education can count as living with the taxpayer","The student automatically qualifies as a qualifying relative only","A student must be under age 19 in every case"],answer:1,explanation:"A temporary absence for education generally is treated as time living with the taxpayer for the qualifying-child residency test. The relationship, age, residency, support and joint-return tests must still be considered."},
{id:"p1q12",part:1,topic:"Itemized deductions",question:"A taxpayer has mortgage interest, qualifying charitable contributions and state income taxes. What is the correct starting point when deciding whether to itemize?",choices:["Deduct every expense without limitation","Compare allowable itemized deductions with the standard deduction and apply relevant limits","Itemize only if the taxpayer owns a business","Claim both the standard deduction and full itemized deductions"],answer:1,explanation:"An individual generally chooses the standard deduction or itemized deductions, whichever is more beneficial, subject to eligibility and limitations. Certain deductions have caps, substantiation rules and other requirements."},
{id:"p1q13",part:1,topic:"Basis and sale of residence",question:"A homeowner sells a principal residence at a gain. Which factor is central to determining whether the home-sale gain exclusion may apply?",choices:["Whether the seller used the home as a principal residence for the required ownership and use periods","Whether the seller has a mortgage at closing","Whether the home was purchased with cash","Whether the buyer is related to the seller"],answer:0,explanation:"The Section 121 exclusion generally requires ownership and use of the home as a principal residence for at least 2 years during the 5-year period ending on the sale date, with exceptions and limitations. Prior exclusions and nonqualified use may matter."},
{id:"p1q14",part:1,topic:"Capital asset basis",question:"An investor sells shares for $9,000. Their adjusted basis is $11,500, and selling costs are $100. What is the realized result before considering special rules?",choices:["$2,400 gain","$2,500 loss","$2,600 loss","$1,400 gain"],answer:2,explanation:"Amount realized is generally proceeds less selling costs: $9,000 − $100 = $8,900. Subtract adjusted basis of $11,500 to get a $2,600 realized loss. Deductibility and character require separate analysis."},
{id:"p1q15",part:1,topic:"Wash sales",question:"An individual sells stock at a loss and buys substantially identical stock 20 days later. What is the general federal income-tax consequence?",choices:["The loss is generally disallowed currently under the wash-sale rule and added to replacement-share basis","The loss becomes tax-exempt","The replacement shares receive zero basis","The loss is automatically a long-term capital loss"],answer:0,explanation:"A purchase of substantially identical securities within the 30-day period before or after a loss sale can trigger the wash-sale rule. The disallowed loss generally adjusts the basis of replacement shares; special rules apply to IRAs and other situations."},
{id:"p1q16",part:1,topic:"Retirement distributions",question:"A taxpayer receives a distribution from a traditional IRA. Which statement is the best general rule?",choices:["Every distribution is tax-free","The taxable portion is generally ordinary income unless an exclusion or basis recovery applies; an additional tax may also apply","The distribution is always capital gain","The distribution is reported only if the taxpayer spends it"],answer:1,explanation:"Traditional IRA distributions are generally included in gross income to the extent they represent untaxed amounts. Nontaxable basis, rollovers, qualified charitable distributions and exceptions to additional tax require separate analysis."},
{id:"p1q17",part:1,topic:"Education credits",question:"A taxpayer claims an education credit for a student. Which item is generally important to determine the eligible credit and amount?",choices:["Only the student's age","Qualified tuition and related expenses, enrollment status, degree program, income limits and prior-year credit usage","The student's preferred career","Whether the college is located in the taxpayer's state"],answer:1,explanation:"Education-credit eligibility depends on the type of credit, qualified expenses, eligible institution, enrollment and degree requirements, modified AGI, dependency rules and limitations such as prior use of the American Opportunity Tax Credit."},
{id:"p1q18",part:1,topic:"Estimated tax",question:"A self-employed individual expects a balance due and has no wage withholding. What should the preparer evaluate during the year?",choices:["Whether estimated tax payments are required to avoid an underpayment penalty","Whether the taxpayer can wait indefinitely because no W-2 exists","Whether estimated payments are deductible business expenses","Whether estimated payments replace the annual return"],answer:0,explanation:"Individuals may need estimated tax payments when withholding and credits will not satisfy applicable payment requirements. Safe-harbor rules, annualized income, payment timing and exceptions should be evaluated."},
{id:"p1q19",part:1,topic:"Passive activity losses",question:"An individual owns a limited partnership interest and has a loss allocated from the partnership. What is a key limitation to consider before deducting the loss?",choices:["The passive activity loss rules, along with basis and at-risk limitations","Only the standard deduction","The wash-sale rule","The household employee tax rules"],answer:0,explanation:"Partnership losses may be limited in sequence by basis, at-risk, passive activity and excess business loss rules, as applicable. A K-1 loss is not automatically deductible in full in the current year."},
{id:"p1q20",part:1,topic:"Net investment income tax",question:"A high-income individual has interest, dividends and capital gains. Which additional federal tax may need to be considered?",choices:["Net Investment Income Tax, depending on modified AGI and net investment income","FUTA tax","Corporate alternative minimum tax in every case","Excise tax on all wages"],answer:0,explanation:"The 3.8% Net Investment Income Tax may apply to the lesser of net investment income or the excess of modified AGI over the applicable threshold. The income categories, threshold, deductions and exceptions must be analyzed."},
// Part 2 — Businesses: advanced scenarios
{id:"p2q11",part:2,topic:"Partnership liabilities and basis",question:"A partner's share of partnership liabilities increases during the year. What is the general potential effect?",choices:["The partner's outside basis may increase, subject to liability-allocation rules","The partner's basis always decreases","The partnership must treat the amount as guaranteed payment","The increase is always taxable wage income"],answer:0,explanation:"A partner's share of partnership liabilities generally enters outside basis under Section 752. Recourse, nonrecourse and qualified nonrecourse financing rules determine allocation. A decrease can be treated as a deemed cash distribution."},
{id:"p2q12",part:2,topic:"S corporation shareholder basis",question:"An S corporation shareholder receives a cash distribution. What must be checked to determine taxability?",choices:["Stock basis and applicable distribution ordering rules, with debt basis treated separately","Only the corporation's gross receipts","The shareholder's mortgage balance","Whether the distribution was made by check or cash"],answer:0,explanation:"S corporation distributions are generally tax-free to the extent of stock basis, subject to ordering rules and accumulated adjustments account concepts. Distributions exceeding basis may produce gain. Debt basis does not generally support tax-free distributions."},
{id:"p2q13",part:2,topic:"Reasonable compensation",question:"An S corporation shareholder performs substantial services and takes distributions but no wages. What issue should the preparer consider?",choices:["Whether reasonable compensation should have been paid as wages subject to employment taxes","Whether all distributions become tax-free wages","Whether the corporation must file Form 1065","Whether the shareholder is automatically an independent contractor"],answer:0,explanation:"An S corporation must generally pay reasonable compensation to a shareholder-employee for services before treating payments as nonwage distributions. Facts, duties, time, comparable pay and revenue sources matter."},
{id:"p2q14",part:2,topic:"C corporation distributions",question:"A C corporation distributes cash to a shareholder. Which tax concept generally determines whether the payment is a dividend?",choices:["Earnings and profits, with applicable distribution ordering rules","The shareholder's outside partnership basis","The corporation's payroll tax deposit schedule","The shareholder's filing status alone"],answer:0,explanation:"Corporate distributions are generally treated first as dividends to the extent of current and accumulated earnings and profits, then as return of capital to basis, and then as gain, subject to special rules."},
{id:"p2q15",part:2,topic:"Depreciation and disposition",question:"A business sells depreciable equipment for more than its adjusted basis. Which issue should be examined?",choices:["Potential depreciation recapture and character under Sections 1245 or 1250","Whether the gain is automatically tax-exempt","Whether the sale belongs on Schedule A only","Whether the buyer must file Form 941"],answer:0,explanation:"Gain on depreciable property may be recharacterized as ordinary income under depreciation-recapture rules, including Section 1245 and Section 1250. Section 1231 netting may also apply after recapture analysis."},
{id:"p2q16",part:2,topic:"Business meal deductions",question:"A sole proprietor pays for a meal with a prospective client while discussing business. What is the appropriate approach?",choices:["Assume 100% deductible because business was discussed","Test the expense against business-meal requirements, applicable percentage limits, substantiation and exclusions","Deduct it as employee wages automatically","Treat it as inventory"],answer:1,explanation:"Business meals generally require a business connection, a present taxpayer or employee, and that the expense is not lavish or extravagant under the circumstances. The usual deduction limitation and documentation requirements apply; entertainment is generally treated differently."},
{id:"p2q17",part:2,topic:"Inventory and cost of goods sold",question:"A retailer's beginning inventory is $30,000, purchases are $120,000, and ending inventory is $25,000. Ignoring other adjustments, what is cost of goods sold?",choices:["$95,000","$125,000","$145,000","$175,000"],answer:1,explanation:"Beginning inventory $30,000 + purchases $120,000 − ending inventory $25,000 = $125,000 cost of goods sold. Freight-in, returns, allowances and other adjustments may change the computation."},
{id:"p2q18",part:2,topic:"Worker classification",question:"A business labels a worker an independent contractor in its agreement. What determines federal employment-tax classification?",choices:["The contract label alone","The actual facts showing the degree of behavioral control, financial control and relationship of the parties","Whether the worker prefers contractor status","Whether payment is made electronically"],answer:1,explanation:"Worker status is determined by the facts and circumstances, not merely the parties' label. IRS common-law analysis considers behavioral control, financial control and the type of relationship."},
{id:"p2q19",part:2,topic:"Qualified business income",question:"A taxpayer receives a Schedule K-1 showing qualified business income. What is true about the Section 199A deduction?",choices:["The deduction is automatic and always equals 20% of all K-1 income","Eligibility and amount depend on taxable income, business type, wage/property limitations and other statutory rules","It is a refundable credit","It reduces self-employment tax directly"],answer:1,explanation:"The Section 199A deduction is subject to multiple limitations, including taxable-income thresholds, specified service trade or business rules and W-2 wage/qualified property limits where applicable. It is a deduction, not a credit or direct SE-tax reduction."},
{id:"p2q20",part:2,topic:"Business loss limitations",question:"A sole proprietor reports a large business loss. Which sequence of limitations may affect the currently deductible amount?",choices:["Basis, at-risk, passive activity and excess business loss rules as applicable to the facts","Only the charitable contribution limits","Only the capital loss limits","No limitations apply to sole proprietors"],answer:0,explanation:"Business losses may be restricted by at-risk rules, passive activity rules and the excess business loss limitation, among other provisions. Entity type and taxpayer facts determine which rules apply and in what order."},
// Part 3 — Representation and procedure: advanced scenarios
{id:"p3q11",part:3,topic:"Audit process",question:"An IRS examiner proposes an adjustment, and the taxpayer disagrees. What is generally a sound next step before the case becomes final?",choices:["Ignore all IRS correspondence","Review the proposed adjustments, supporting workpapers and notice, then evaluate available administrative response and appeal rights","Destroy records that disagree with the examiner","File Form 2848 as an automatic appeal"],answer:1,explanation:"The taxpayer or authorized representative should understand the proposed changes, deadlines and procedural posture, assemble relevant evidence, and evaluate the appropriate response or Appeals route. The notice controls important deadlines."},
{id:"p3q12",part:3,topic:"Power of attorney scope",question:"A Form 2848 lists only one tax year and one tax form. What is the general scope of the authorization?",choices:["It automatically covers every tax type and every future year","It is limited by the tax matters, periods and representative authority stated and accepted on the form","It authorizes the representative to sign any legal document","It transfers the taxpayer's liability to the representative"],answer:1,explanation:"A power of attorney is limited to the matters and periods specified, subject to IRS processing and applicable rules. Representatives should verify that the authorization covers the issue before acting."},
{id:"p3q13",part:3,topic:"Collection alternatives",question:"A taxpayer cannot pay a tax balance in full but may be able to make monthly payments. Which option should be evaluated?",choices:["An installment agreement, after reviewing eligibility, compliance and ability to pay","An automatic discharge of the liability","A request to erase the assessment by filing Form 8821","A tax-free distribution from an unrelated corporation"],answer:0,explanation:"An installment agreement may permit payment over time. The taxpayer generally must be compliant with required filing and payment obligations, and fees, interest, penalties and collection terms may apply."},
{id:"p3q14",part:3,topic:"Offer in compromise",question:"Which statement best describes an Offer in Compromise based on doubt as to collectibility?",choices:["The IRS must accept any offer below the tax owed","It may settle a liability for less than the full amount when the legal and financial criteria are met and the offer is accepted","It is the same as an installment agreement","It is available only to corporations"],answer:1,explanation:"An OIC based on doubt as to collectibility generally considers reasonable collection potential and other financial factors. Acceptance is not automatic; filing compliance, required payments and other eligibility rules matter."},
{id:"p3q15",part:3,topic:"Refund claims",question:"A taxpayer discovers an error that caused an overpayment on a previously filed individual return. What is the usual correction route?",choices:["File an amended return using Form 1040-X, subject to refund-claim deadlines and rules","File Form 2848 only","Submit a new original return without identifying the prior return","Wait until the IRS audits the taxpayer"],answer:0,explanation:"Form 1040-X is generally used to amend an individual income tax return. A refund claim must meet statutory timing requirements, and not every error requires an amended return."},
{id:"p3q16",part:3,topic:"Penalty relief",question:"A taxpayer requests reasonable-cause penalty relief. Which evidence is most useful?",choices:["A general statement that taxes are confusing","A contemporaneous explanation and records showing the facts, dates, actions taken and circumstances that prevented compliance","A claim that penalties are always optional","A copy of an unrelated bank statement only"],answer:1,explanation:"Reasonable cause is fact-specific. Relevant documentation should explain what happened, how it affected compliance, the taxpayer's ordinary business care and prudence, and actions taken once the obstacle ended."},
{id:"p3q17",part:3,topic:"Confidentiality",question:"A third party calls a tax practitioner asking for a client's return information but has no authorization. What should the practitioner generally do?",choices:["Provide the information if the caller knows the client's address","Protect the client's confidential tax information and verify valid authorization before disclosure","Provide only the refund amount","Send the information to the caller's employer"],answer:1,explanation:"Federal law and professional obligations protect taxpayer information. A practitioner should verify an applicable authorization or legal basis before disclosing confidential tax information."},
{id:"p3q18",part:3,topic:"Statute of limitations",question:"A taxpayer files a valid return and later makes a payment. Which statement about the collection statute is generally correct?",choices:["The general collection period is commonly 10 years from assessment, subject to statutory suspensions and extensions","It always expires three years after filing","It begins only when the taxpayer receives a levy","It can never be suspended"],answer:0,explanation:"The general collection statute is commonly 10 years from assessment. Bankruptcy, collection due process proceedings, offers in compromise and other events can suspend or affect the period. Compute the actual date from account transcripts and applicable rules."},
{id:"p3q19",part:3,topic:"Appeals independence",question:"Why is the IRS Independent Office of Appeals distinct from the examination function?",choices:["It is intended to provide an impartial administrative forum to resolve disputes without litigation when possible","It prepares the original audit report","It acts as the taxpayer's private attorney","It has authority to rewrite the Internal Revenue Code"],answer:0,explanation:"Appeals is intended to resolve tax controversies impartially based on hazards of litigation and applicable law, separate from the examination function. The precise procedure depends on the type of case and notice."},
{id:"p3q20",part:3,topic:"Practitioner duties",question:"A practitioner discovers a material error in a client's previously filed return. Which response is most consistent with professional standards?",choices:["Ignore the error if the IRS has not noticed","Promptly advise the client of the error and consequences and discuss appropriate corrective steps, while observing applicable law and professional duties","Alter the practitioner's copy and say nothing","Contact the IRS pretending to be the client"],answer:1,explanation:"Circular 230 §10.21 requires a practitioner who knows a client has not complied with revenue laws or made an error or omission to advise the client promptly of the noncompliance, error or omission and its consequences. The practitioner should not mislead the IRS or client."}
];
